      "payload": "{\"model_calls\":1,\"phase\":\"preparation\",\"plan\":{\"A\":\"Research successor-generation improvements without changing search order or state identity. Precompute legal neighbors to remove repeated coordinate division and boundary work. Compare repeated box scans against a reusable cell-to-box-index scratch map populated once per expanded state and cleared through touched cells. Skip sorting on walking moves; for pushes, replace bubble sorting with single-element insertion within the reference's sorted prefix, preserving its separate final box slot. Avoid full goal scans for walking successors, while handling the initial state and pushes correctly. Prioritize small, independently measured changes; preprocessing and scratch-map overhead may outweigh savings here. Preserve general board and box-count support rather than assuming dimensions observed in correctness cases.\",\"B\":\"Research exact visited-state lookup and allocation efficiency, keeping A's search semantics unchanged. First restore the stored-hash equality check before memcmp. Then investigate better hash mixing and a cache-friendly open-addressed table with full-key collision checks. Construct candidate keys in reusable scratch storage and allocate persistent states only after determining that they are new. Compare the existing prelinked free lists against correctly aligned bump allocation with stable parent references. More compact state identifiers are optional, not a prerequisite. Preserve discovery order, exact player coordinates, and the reference box representation. Never use hash-only equality or merge player positions by reachable region. Return the fastest validated variant, including a simpler variant if the larger redesign adds overhead.\",\"C\":\"Research sound deadlock pruning while retaining movement-by-movement BFS and its existing ordering. The source already computes union reverse-push reachability, so merely recreating static dead squares is not a new optimization. Start with a cheap post-push check for a fully occupied 2-by-2 square of walls/boxes containing an off-goal box. Then investigate per-goal reverse-push reachability and reject configurations lacking a matching from every box to a distinct reachable goal, treating other boxes as absent during reachability preprocessing. Use general representations or checked fallbacks for larger boards and goal sets. Prove each rejection cannot remove a solution; frozen boxes already on goals must not trigger an unsound rejection. Avoid push-count BFS, arbitrary search limits, and speculative freeze rules. Measure whether reduced exploration repays preprocessing and checking costs.\",\"G\":\"Integrate only validated contributions from A/B/C, retaining the baseline and best standalone child as alternatives. Establish a shared contract for candidate keys, box ordering, scratch occupancy, parent references, and frontier order before combining changes. Measure incremental combinations with the unchanged automatic harness and existing budget; gains are not additive, and a combined program may be slower. Compare exact output, including movement capitalization, chosen path, newline, and termination behavior—not just solution length. Ensure discovering a solution takes precedence over an empty next frontier, especially after pruning. Review general-input correctness and checked representation limits beyond the four observed cases. Keep compiler, target, tests, oracle, permissions, and budgets unchanged. Select the fastest validated measured program, preferring simplicity when timing differences are inconclusive.\",\"analysis\":\"Only Problem C and src/sokoban.c are the optimization target. The baseline passed four cases and averaged 4.812 ms, with samples spanning 4.689–4.988 ms; there is no profile or child measurement establishing a bottleneck. Favor low-overhead serial improvements on the four-core ARM64 target before introducing parallel synchronization. Source-derived opportunities are repeated successor work, hash-chain comparisons and allocation, and unnecessary exploration. Exact trace compatibility is delicate: moves are generated right/left/up/down and each next layer is prepended, which is not ordinary up/down/left/right FIFO traversal. Also, the sorter orders only c[1] through c[n_boxes-1], leaving the final box slot separate. Changing traversal or fully canonicalizing boxes can therefore change duplicate handling and the selected trace. The proposed trees separate expansion cost, storage cost, and sound search-space reduction; all benefits remain hypotheses until measured.\"},\"request_sha256\":\"d7875dddcb2d39116c9cbaacf302ba25b0084bf5d86af71d76ec0cc4e8fe0e14\",\"response\":{\"data\":{\"A\":\"Research successor-generation improvements without changing search order or state identity. Precompute legal neighbors to remove repeated coordinate division and boundary work. Compare repeated box scans against a reusable cell-to-box-index scratch map populated once per expanded state and cleared through touched cells. Skip sorting on walking moves; for pushes, replace bubble sorting with single-element insertion within the reference's sorted prefix, preserving its separate final box slot. Avoid full goal scans for walking successors, while handling the initial state and pushes correctly. Prioritize small, independently measured changes; preprocessing and scratch-map overhead may outweigh savings here. Preserve general board and box-count support rather than assuming dimensions observed in correctness cases.\",\"B\":\"Research exact visited-state lookup and allocation efficiency, keeping A's search semantics unchanged. First restore the stored-hash equality check before memcmp. Then investigate better hash mixing and a cache-friendly open-addressed table with full-key collision checks. Construct candidate keys in reusable scratch storage and allocate persistent states only after determining that they are new. Compare the existing prelinked free lists against correctly aligned bump allocation with stable parent references. More compact state identifiers are optional, not a prerequisite. Preserve discovery order, exact player coordinates, and the reference box representation. Never use hash-only equality or merge player positions by reachable region. Return the fastest validated variant, including a simpler variant if the larger redesign adds overhead.\",\"C\":\"Research sound deadlock pruning while retaining movement-by-movement BFS and its existing ordering. The source already computes union reverse-push reachability, so merely recreating static dead squares is not a new optimization. Start with a cheap post-push check for a fully occupied 2-by-2 square of walls/boxes containing an off-goal box. Then investigate per-goal reverse-push reachability and reject configurations lacking a matching from every box to a distinct reachable goal, treating other boxes as absent during reachability preprocessing. Use general representations or checked fallbacks for larger boards and goal sets. Prove each rejection cannot remove a solution; frozen boxes already on goals must not trigger an unsound rejection. Avoid push-count BFS, arbitrary search limits, and speculative freeze rules. Measure whether reduced exploration repays preprocessing and checking costs.\",\"G\":\"Integrate only validated contributions from A/B/C, retaining the baseline and best standalone child as alternatives. Establish a shared contract for candidate keys, box ordering, scratch occupancy, parent references, and frontier order before combining changes. Measure incremental combinations with the unchanged automatic harness and existing budget; gains are not additive, and a combined program may be slower. Compare exact output, including movement capitalization, chosen path, newline, and termination behavior—not just solution length. Ensure discovering a solution takes precedence over an empty next frontier, especially after pruning. Review general-input correctness and checked representation limits beyond the four observed cases. Keep compiler, target, tests, oracle, permissions, and budgets unchanged. Select the fastest validated measured program, preferring simplicity when timing differences are inconclusive.\",\"analysis\":\"Only Problem C and src/sokoban.c are the optimization target. The baseline passed four cases and averaged 4.812 ms, with samples spanning 4.689–4.988 ms; there is no profile or child measurement establishing a bottleneck. Favor low-overhead serial improvements on the four-core ARM64 target before introducing parallel synchronization. Source-derived opportunities are repeated successor work, hash-chain comparisons and allocation, and unnecessary exploration. Exact trace compatibility is delicate: moves are generated right/left/up/down and each next layer is prepended, which is not ordinary up/down/left/right FIFO traversal. Also, the sorter orders only c[1] through c[n_boxes-1], leaving the final box slot separate. Changing traversal or fully canonicalizing boxes can therefore change duplicate handling and the selected trace. The proposed trees separate expansion cost, storage cost, and sound search-space reduction; all benefits remain hypotheses until measured.\"},\"error\":null,\"infrastructure_error\":false,\"model\":\"gpt-6-astra\",\"nonce\":\"d62d859bf18a4ce094b3aaf4a5bc2ff5\",\"protocol\":\"hpc-forest-codex-mailbox-v1\",\"reasoning_effort\":\"max\",\"request_sha256\":\"d7875dddcb2d39116c9cbaacf302ba25b0084bf5d86af71d76ec0cc4e8fe0e14\",\"session_id\":\"aa970cf8bf5d4c1aaa0fdabb21d73a72\",\"usage\":{\"cached_tokens_included_in_input\":true,\"codex_jsonl_sha256\":\"a787e51a48e97d2ad0f0271e653bbe2529a41b4d38af842ed578f462bda37a5a\",\"events\":[{\"cache_write_input_tokens\":0,\"cached_input_tokens\":0,\"input_tokens\":13726,\"output_tokens\":7520,\"reasoning_output_tokens\":6709}],\"llm_calls\":1,\"model_requested\":\"gpt-6-astra\",\"reasoning_effort_requested\":\"max\",\"token_accounting\":\